Relevant persisted fields (there is NO `membershipTier` field on the entity — tier is derived in the mapper; there IS a stored `namesakeCount` and `householdId`):
```java
@Column(name = "registration_date", columnDefinition = "DATE")
private LocalDate registrationDate;

@Column(name = "customer_code")
private String customerCode;

@Column(name = "household_id")
private String householdId;

@Column(name = "namesake_count")
private Integer namesakeCount;
```
Accessors:
```java
public String getHouseholdId() { return this.householdId; }
public void setHouseholdId(String householdId) { this.householdId = householdId; }

public Integer getNamesakeCount() { return this.namesakeCount; }
public void setNamesakeCount(Integer namesakeCount) { this.namesakeCount = namesakeCount; }
```
Owner extends `Person` (which provides `firstName`/`lastName`). Other fields: `address`, `city`, `telephone`, `email`, `pets`. `toString()` includes `householdId` and `namesakeCount`.

If you add a stored household member count, follow the `namesakeCount` pattern: `@Column(name = "...")` field + getter/setter here, plus mark it `ignore = true` in `OwnerMapper.toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` (see section 4/mapper below).

---

## 4. OwnerRepository interface

### `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/OwnerRepository.java`
All methods:
```java
Collection<Owner> findByLastName(String lastName) throws DataAccessException;
Page<Owner> findByLastName(String lastName, Pageable pageable) throws DataAccessException;
Owner findById(int id) throws DataAccessException;
void save(Owner owner) throws DataAccessException;
Collection<Owner> findAll() throws DataAccessException;
Page<Owner> findAll(Pageable pageable) throws DataAccessException;
int count() throws DataAccessException;
void delete(Owner owner) throws DataAccessException;
```
There is NO `findByHouseholdId`. To count household members you would either iterate `findAll()` filtering by `getHouseholdId()` (the pattern used everywhere else, e.g. `CountNamesakes`, `AssignCustomerCode`), or add a new query method here.

Note in `OwnerMapper` the `toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` mapping already ignores derived/persisted fields (line 70-75):
```java
@Mapping(target = "id", ignore = true)
@Mapping(target = "pets", ignore = true)
@Mapping(target = "customerCode", ignore = true)
@Mapping(target = "householdId", ignore = true)
@Mapping(target = "namesakeCount", ignore = true)
Owner toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto ownerDto);
```

---

## 5. Tests related to membership tier / owner creation

### `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` (the failing/target test)
```java
/** tier-gold: membershipTier is 'GOLD' when the owner's household has 3+ members after this
 * create. Build a shared household (same lastName + address, sharesHousehold) and assert the third
 * member is GOLD. */
class AcceptanceTest extends AcceptanceBase {

	@Test
	void coreGoldForThreeMemberHousehold() throws Exception {
		String lastName = uniqueLastName();
		String address = uniqueAddress();
		for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
			ObjectNode m = ownerNode();
			m.put("lastName", lastName);
			m.put("address", address);
			m.put("sharesHousehold", true);
			createOwnerOk(m);
		}
		ObjectNode third = ownerNode();
		third.put("lastName", lastName);
		third.put("address", address);
		third.put("sharesHousehold", true);
		int id = createOwnerOk(third);
		getOwner(id).andExpect(jsonPath("$.membershipTier").value("GOLD"));
	}
}
```

### `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` (base harness)
Read in full above. Key helpers: `ownerNode()` (fully-unique valid owner, note it does NOT set an email), `createOwnerOk(body)` (POST, expects 2xx, returns id), `getOwner(id)`, `uniqueLastName()`, `uniqueAddress()`. Tests run `@Transactional` (rolled back) as `@WithMockUser` admin with roles `OWNER_ADMIN, VET_ADMIN, ADMIN`. Note: `ownerNode()` omits email, so under the current SILVER rule these owners would be BRONZE — the GOLD rule the test wants must take precedence over the email/namesake SILVER/BRONZE rule based on household size (3+).

I searched for other tier/owner-creation tests; `AcceptanceTest` is the only test referencing membership tier or GOLD. No unit test exists for `OwnerMapper.membershipTier`.

---

## 6. Where the tier computation lives (BRONZE/SILVER rules)

The ONLY tier computation is `OwnerMapper.membershipTier(Owner)` in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java` lines 54-58 (quoted in section 1). Rules today:
- `SILVER` if `namesakeCount == 0` AND email present.
- `BRONZE` otherwise.
- `GOLD`: not implemented anywhere.

There is no separate service/strategy class, no enum, and no household-size input to this method. The DTOs (`OwnerDto`, `OwnerFieldsDto`) are code-generated by the openapi-generator-maven-plugin from `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml` into package `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto` (no hand-written source files; confirmed via `pom.xml` lines 370-388, `modelNameSuffix=Dto`). `OwnerFieldsDto` includes the write-only `sharesHousehold` boolean (openapi.yml lines 2048-2056, accessed via `request.getSharesHousehold()`).

### Implementation note for adding GOLD
`OwnerMapper.toOwnerDto(Owner)` has no repository access, so to compute "household has 3+ members" you have two viable routes consistent with the codebase:
1. Post-mapping enrichment in `RespondWithOwner` and `RespondWithOwnerCreated` (both already inject `OwnerRepository` and mutate the DTO after mapping — like `bulkSignupWarning`), counting `findAll()` owners with the same non-null `householdId` and overriding the tier to GOLD when >= 3. This keeps the mapper pure but requires overriding in two response functions (and any list endpoints if they must reflect GOLD too).
2. Store a household member count on the `Owner` entity (mirror `namesakeCount`: add column + getter/setter in `Owner.java`, ignore it in `toOwner`, populate it in a new create-flow function, and read it in `membershipTier`). Because `AssignHousehold` runs during create and stamps all members, a count computed at read time (route 1) is simplest and matches the test which reads via GET after all three exist.

